<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Catepanate of Italy</h1><p class="st-meta">965 CE – 1071 CE (approx.) · capital Bari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Byzantine province of southern Italy governed from Bari by a catepan, the last stronghold of the Eastern Empire in Italy until the Normans took Bari in 1071.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 965–970 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> combined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/theme-of-longobardia">Longobardia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a> under a single high-ranking governor, the catepan, based at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a>. The change answered threats from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I's</a> campaigns in the south and from Arab raids.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The catepanate governed a mixed population of Latin-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Lombards</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, with Greek monasteries flourishing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. Local revolts were frequent: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melus-of-bari">Melus of Bari</a> rebelled in 1009 and again in 1017 with Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, but the catepan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-boioannes">Basil Boioannes</a> crushed them at Cannae in 1018 and founded new towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troia-apulia">Troia</a>. The province also served as a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maniakes">George Maniakes's</a> expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1038.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Normans, first hired as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, conquered the province piece by piece from the 1040s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a> in April 1071 after a long siege, ending Byzantine rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Greek religious and cultural influence lasted much longer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salento">Salento</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/catepanate-of-italy.jpg" alt="Catepanate of Italy" width="320" height="298"><figcaption>Image: Contributor175 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catapanate_of_italy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>965 CE – 1071 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Basil Boioannes, Argyrus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Catepanate of Bari</dd></dl></aside></div>
